PUBLIC RESPONSE: F

The circumflex in French is an historical / etymological diacritic, not a stylistic prestige mark. The functions that are documented in the history of the orthography are essentially those of an historical accent:

* the classic etymological marking of a vowel that is long because a following consonant, most often /s/ but also /l/ and a few others, has been lost in the spoken language and is still visible in the written form, forêt < forest, hôte < hospes, etc. That is H and the material basis of G/I;
* the consequent historical marking of vowel length in Old / Middle French, D, and the modern reflex of that length in a closed / open quality contrast, e.g. côte /kɔt/ 'quotation' ~ côte /kot/ 'coast', A;
* the use of the same spelling distinction to keep apart homophones / homographs that would otherwise be written identically, e.g. du /dü/ 'of the' ~ dû /dü/ 'owed', sur /syʀ/ ~ sûr /syʀ/ 'sure', B;
* the same loss-of-consonant mechanism is regularly described as the monophthongisation / reduction of an earlier diphthong / hiatus sequence produced by the lengthening, and it is the conventional formulation of the sibilant-loss rule, H, G and I.

The circumflex is therefore a record of phonological history – loss of /s/ and other consonants, former length and the consequent modern quality contrast and homophone disambiguation – and never a normative stylistic device to make a word look more learned or prestigious. C is also a misleading formulation: the circumflex is not a marker of the phonetic value [o] and it is not limited to Classical Latin etymons; it marks historical length / consonantal loss on a, e, i, o, u in Latinate and non-Latinate material alike.

