* C. is not an independent function but a Latinate instance of the same length/open-vowel development, e.g. hô, nôtre < nostrum, vôtre < vestrum, pôle < polus. The circumflex occurs on a, e, i, o, u alike, so it is not limited to [o] or to Classical Latin.

All of those are documented in the normative tradition from the 16th c. onward and in the successive Académie codifications.

A sociolinguistic prestige / learned look motivation has never been codified. The modern perception that ^ makes a word look more learned, elegant or archaic is a secondary folk perception / hypercorrection of an etymological spelling, not a prescriptive orthographic function of the sign. No Académie ruling or historical grammar introduces or retains the circumflex to confer prestige or style.

That leaves F as the single option with no historical orthographic attestation.

